Predicting silent failures of learned inferenceunder restricted access: what measurement physicsand prior information explain Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2610.28155v1 [stat.ML] 20 Oct 2026 Predicting silent failures of learned inference under restricted access: what measurement physics and prior information explain Mario Gianni Affiliation: School of Computer Science and Informatics, University of Liverpool, UK Email: Mario.Gianni@liverpool.ac.uk ORCID 0000-0001-5410-2377 18 October 2026 Abstract Learned priors are used to reconstruct regions that measurements barely reach, and to report uncertainty there. They can fail silently: the reported interval excludes the truth while every ground-truth-free diagnostic stays within its noise. We call such errors the blind set and ask whether they can be predicted before the target data are seen. For a correctly specified linear-Gaussian model averaged over the prior, the risk of a silent failure factorises exactly into non-detection and non-coverage, which does not hold for learned systems in general; we build a score that approximates this reference by linearising a VAE prior. In a prospective study on real X-ray CT objects (2DeteCT) under simulated restricted access, with predictions committed by hash before any trial, the score ranked the realised blindness of two learned inference systems (latent Laplace and ensemble Kalman inversion) with Spearman ρ = 0.79 ho=0.79 and 0.84 0.84 over 240 pre-specified errors. Simple structural predictors would also have passed these tests. Sequential post-hoc analyses, each specified before its own computation, show that under bounded reach the learned score gave no detectable improvement over a physics-based baseline, in rankings or trial-level log scores. At the weakest attenuation studied, prior information improved rankings over the physics-based baseline, most clearly for ensemble Kalman inversion, but a Gaussian prior did as well and the gain was not detectable at the trial level. The score is not a calibrated probability. Silent failures can be anticipated here, but their structure follows mainly from measurement access and the decision quantity rather than from the learned prior. Keywords: uncertainty quantification; silent failure; learned priors; variational autoencoder; Bayesian inverse problems; computed tomography; restricted access; pre-registration. 1 Introduction Reconstructing what cannot be observed directly, such as the interior of a sealed component or a region a sensor cannot reach, is increasingly done with learned priors [ 4 ] , and decisions are taken on the uncertainty these systems report. Such a system can fail in two ways. It can be uncertain and say so: its reported uncertainty is wide where its evidence is thin. Or it can be confidently wrong : the reported uncertainty excludes the truth, while every check it can run without ground truth (residuals, consistency across measurement subsets, plausibility of the fitted latent state) stays within its noise. The second failure looks, from inside the system, exactly like a well-supported inference. Each ingredient of this failure is established. Misspecification that the data cannot detect can be bounded or made harmless to estimators [ 11 , 2 ] ; prior-data conflict checks find misspecification that does reach the data [ 7 ] . Priors mismatched to the truth can destroy the coverage of credible sets in inverse problems [ 15 , 10 ] , including under partial observation [ 9 ] . Learned reconstructions can place false structure in components the measurements do not determine [ 3 ] ; learned uncertainty degrades under distribution shift [ 16 ] ; conformal methods guarantee coverage only on average and under exchangeability [ 1 ] ; and misspecified models can be precise yet inaccurate with little residual warning [ 5 ] . What is not established is whether the conjunction —errors both undetected and outside the reported uncertainty—can be predicted in advance for a learned non-linear system, from the model, the operator, the diagnostic suite and the noise alone. The blind set differs from each of these notions: non-coverage alone may be flagged by a diagnostic, an undetectable perturbation may leave the decision quantity unchanged, and prior-data conflict concerns misfit that the data do reveal. The blind set is their intersection, defined for a stated decision quantity and diagnostic suite. We separate three claims: (a) blind errors can be predicted prospectively; (b) local linearisation of learned inference provides an effective predictor; (c) learned-prior geometry contributes information that simpler predictors lack. The evidence supports (a) in the tested setting, gives qualified support to (b), and does not establish (c) (Section 8 ). The pre-specified tests show that a score computed before any trial ranks realised blindness. They do not separate it from structural baselines: the indicator of the unreached region and the error’s effect on the decision quantity alone reach Spearman ρ = 0.52 ho=0.52 –0.66 and 0.57–0.70 under bounded reach, so they too would have met the pre-specified threshold. Everything we conclude about what makes blindness predictable comes from sequential post-hoc analyses, each specified in writing before its own computation but designed with earlier results known (Table 1 ). Linear-Gaussian pilots of the blind set are reported in a companion technical note [ 8 ] ; the present paper does not rely on them. Our contributions are of three kinds, which we keep apart. 1. Formulation and reference. The blind set and its population-averaged risk (Section 2 ), and an exact result for the linear-Gaussian case (Proposition 1 ): averaged over the prior, non-detection and non-coverage are independent for any detector, and the posterior spread is the correct scale. The result follows from a standard property of Gaussian conditioning; what it adds is its application to the blindness risk. 2. A computational procedure. A prospective risk-ranking score that approximates this reference by linearising a learned prior (Section 3 ). We offer no new theory of why learned uncertainty fails. 3. Empirical findings about which information suffices to anticipate silent failure under different access regimes (Sections 5 – 7 ): a prospective test on real CT objects with two learned inference systems; comparisons with physics-based, Gaussian and structural predictors at the level of rankings and of individual trials; and a full record of what was fixed when, of failed configurations and of deviations (Appendix A ). Part of what we observe follows directly from the measurement null space, the size of an error and the sensitivity of the decision quantity; Proposition 1 makes this precise. The experiments add what does not follow from these alone: how often calibrated learned systems were silently wrong beyond reach; that, at the level of individual trials, the learned score gave no detectable predictive improvement over a physics-based score; that under weak but non-zero access prior information improves rankings, though not in a way specific to the learned prior nor as a detectable gain in trial-level predictive performance; and that a score computed before any target data, from a single surrogate, ranked the blindness of two different inference algorithms. 2 Formulation and a linear-Gaussian reference Decision quantity, interval and detector. The object is an image x ∈ ℝ n x\in\mathbb{R}^{n} and the measurements are y = A ​ x + ε y=Ax+\varepsilon . The decision quantity is linear, q ⁡ ( x ) = c ⊤ ​ x q(x)=c^{	op}x , with c = 𝟏 R / | R | c=\mathbf{1}_{R}/|R| the normalised indicator of a disc R R , so q q is the mean attenuation over R R . An inference system reports an estimate q ^ ​ ( y ) \hat{q}(y) , a sp